{"archetypes":[{"name":"schema-drift","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The issue body uses nested-directory paths (plan-review/round-<N>/plan-after.txt, plan-review/round-cursor.txt) while the plan document switches to flat top-level naming (plan-after-round-<N>.txt, plan-review-round-cursor.txt) — an unresolved naming-convention split that will make consumers produce or read files from wrong locations, and that conflicts with the existing plan-review/round-<N>/ subdirectory convention already present in plan-review-loop.sh and design-log-publish.md.","prompt_body":"Read `skills/design/scripts/plan-review-loop.sh` lines 90-110 and 620-640 to confirm the existing `plan-review/round-<N>/` subdirectory convention. Then compare every artifact path named in the plan document (e.g., `plan-after-round-<N>.txt`, `plan-review-round-cursor.txt`, `assessor-verdict-round-<N>.txt`) against (a) the corresponding paths named in the issue description inside the `reviewer_description` block and (b) the harvest query at `scripts/design-log-publish.sh:274` plus the `plan-review/round-<N>/` allowlist documented in `scripts/design-log-publish.md:23-24`. Flag every artifact whose location differs between these three sources, noting which scripts will produce the file at one path and which will attempt to consume it from a different path. Also check whether the flat top-level naming escapes the `plan-review/round-<N>/` allowlist harvest, causing per-round artifacts to be missing from the design log. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"seam-integrity","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan assumes several integration points exist in specific forms in the codebase (SKILL.md line 734 literal --round-num 1, plan-review-loop.sh --round-num N acceptance, dispatch-with-waterfall.sh --require-result-pattern, append-tool-failure.sh argv shape), but any deviation between assumption and reality will break the new integration without a compile-time signal.","prompt_body":"For each integration assumption in the plan, verify the actual codebase state: check `skills/design/SKILL.md` around line 734 for the literal `--round-num 1` argument and confirm it is in a `plan-review-loop.sh` call rather than somewhere else; check `skills/design/scripts/plan-review-loop.sh` for the `--round-num` flag in its argv parser; check `scripts/dispatch-with-waterfall.sh` for `--require-result-pattern` and confirm the flag name and ERE validation match the plan's assumed behavior exactly; check `scripts/append-tool-failure.sh` for its argv contract and confirm the call signature described in `assess-plan-round.sh`'s plan section matches; check whether `scripts/render-final-summary.sh` accepts any `--outcome` token string or maintains an enumeration. For each point where the plan assumes a behavior that is absent, different, or gated differently than stated, flag the specific plan step that will fail. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"tally-math","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan defines a 3/2/1/0-voter WORSE-majority rule but the edge-cases section states '0-2-1 (0 BETTER + 2 TIE + 1 WORSE) → NOT_WORSE', which contradicts the rule (successful=3, worse_count=1 > better_count=0 satisfies the strict WORSE > BETTER condition on 3 voters), revealing an ambiguity in whether TIE votes count toward 'successful' for purposes of applying the rule.","prompt_body":"Extract the exact tally rule definition from `tally-plan-assessor.sh`'s plan section and enumerate every named distribution in the edge-cases section of both the plan document and the issue description. For each distribution, mechanically apply the plan's stated rule (WORSE-majority when: successful==3 and worse_count > better_count; OR successful==2 and worse_count==2; OR successful==1 and worse_count==1) and compare the result to the expected outcome stated in the edge cases, explicitly resolving whether TIE is counted in `successful` or excluded. Flag every distribution where the rule definition and stated expected outcome are inconsistent, including the '0-2-1' case, the '1-1-1' case, and the '0-3-0 all-TIE' case. Also check whether the original problem statement's 'majority vote' language (WORSE > BETTER strict inequality) aligns with the 3/2/1/0-voter degraded-panel sub-rules when TIEs are present. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
